xpilot-ref patch 0006: optional random number, debris and cannon trace.

This patch modifies XPilot 4.5.5 (GPL-2.0-or-later, Copyright (C) 1991-2010
by Bjoern Stabell, Ken Ronny Schouten, Bert Gijsbers, Dick Balaska et al.).
It is a derivative work distributed under the same license.

Why: so that a reimplementation can replay a C game with the C server's own
random numbers and compare the results exactly (item packs and drops, missile
aim errors, explosion debris counts, cannons...).  Applies on top of 0005.
Every rfrac() and randomMT() call in src/server goes through a wrapper in
server.c (macros at the end of src/server/proto.h; the two declarations in
src/common/commonproto.h are parenthesized so the macros cannot touch them),
Make_debris (play.c) learns its call site, and Receive_keyboard_frame
(netserver.c) reports each keyboard update.  Inert unless the environment variable
XPILOT_TRACE_RAND is set; then it prints to stderr:
  XPRAND func file:line f|i value      every draw, in order; value is the
                                       32-bit randomMT() number, an rfrac()
                                       (f) is value / 2^32
  XPDEBRIS frame func file:line x y vx vy id team type mass status color
           radius min_debris max_debris min_dir max_dir min_speed max_speed
           min_life max_life           every Make_debris call (its caller,
                                       its arguments, floats as %a)
  XPDEB loop frame index type id cx cy vx vy mass life status
                                       after each Update_objects(), every
                                       spark or debris object that is not
                                       owner-immune (explosion debris)
  XPCANNON loop frame index dead_time damaged tractor_count used
           emergency_shield_left phasing_left
                                       after each Update_objects(), every
                                       cannon that is not idle
  XPKEYS frame id k1,k2,...|-          every keyboard update a player's
                                       client sends (netserver.c), with the
                                       keys_t numbers it holds down
Game logic and the random sequence are unchanged.

